# Variables the monitoring compose project reads. Copy to `infra/.env`.
#
#   docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.monitoring.yaml --env-file infra/.env up -d
#
# Four of them are read from the application's own host `.env` as well
# (DB_USER, DB_PASS, DB_NAME, VALKEY_PASSWORD) because the exporters connect
# with the same credentials the application does. Keep the two files in step,
# or point `--env-file` at both.


# =============================================================================
# Exposure
# =============================================================================

# OPTIONAL — host address Grafana, Prometheus, Alertmanager and node-exporter
# publish on. The same private interface the API, Postgres and both Valkeys
# already use; 127.0.0.1 would keep them off the network entirely.
MON_BIND_IP=192.168.180.1

# OPTIONAL — published ports on that address.
GRAFANA_BIND_PORT=10300
PROM_BIND_PORT=10090
ALERTMANAGER_BIND_PORT=10093


# =============================================================================
# Grafana
# =============================================================================

# REQUIRED — the initial admin password. Compose refuses to start without it
# rather than falling back to `admin`, which is how a default password ends up
# on a private network for a year.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# OPTIONAL — the admin account's name.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin

# OPTIONAL — how Grafana addresses itself in the links it generates. Nothing
# proxies it, so this is the address a browser reaches it on.
GRAFANA_ROOT_URL=https://grafana.umalt.app


# =============================================================================
# Prometheus
# =============================================================================

# OPTIONAL — how long samples are kept. Fifteen days at these intervals is a
# few hundred megabytes, on the same disk as the database.
PROM_RETENTION=15d

# OPTIONAL — a second ceiling, so a cardinality mistake cannot fill the disk
# before the time-based limit would have trimmed it.
PROM_RETENTION_SIZE=8GB


# =============================================================================
# Logs — VictoriaLogs and Vector
# =============================================================================
#
# The log half of the stack. Three sources reach VictoriaLogs through Vector:
# container stdout, the nginx files, and the per-sync scrape logs under
# ../log. Why this and not Loki is argued in docs/LOGS-PLAN.md §2.

# OPTIONAL — published port on MON_BIND_IP. Grafana reaches VictoriaLogs over
# the compose network and does not use this; it is for a person with a browser
# — VictoriaLogs serves its own log-explorer UI here — and for the
# verification queries in docs/LOGS-PLAN.md §8.
VLOGS_BIND_PORT=10428

# OPTIONAL — how long lines are kept. Thirty days deliberately matches
# SYNC_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS in the application's own .env, so a sync run's lines
# and the file they came from expire together.
#
# LINES OLDER THAN THIS ARE DROPPED AT INGESTION rather than on a later sweep,
# and the insert is still answered 200 with an empty body — so replaying an
# old file stores nothing and says nothing. That is what
# `vl_rows_dropped_total{reason="too_small_timestamp"}` counts, and why the
# nginx source reads from the end of the file rather than back-filling.
VLOGS_RETENTION=30d

# OPTIONAL — a second ceiling, the PROM_RETENTION_SIZE of this store: a
# cardinality mistake or a chatty deploy must not fill the disk that also
# holds pg_data before the time-based limit would have trimmed it. Dropped a
# whole per-day partition at a time, oldest first.
#
# NOT A HARD WALL, and the documentation is explicit about three limits: usage
# can exceed it between two checks, at least the last TWO DAYS are kept
# whatever this says, and below ~20 % free disk VictoriaLogs goes read-only.
# `WhiskyDiskFilling` stays the real guard, exactly as infra/README.md already
# says about PROM_RETENTION_SIZE.
VLOGS_RETENTION_SIZE=8GB

# OPTIONAL — the container's memory limit, and the number VictoriaLogs
# actually plans against: VictoriaMetrics components size their caches from a
# share of the memory they can see, and they read the cgroup limit. Raising
# the ingest rate without raising this is the OOM-kill path — the same
# relationship `--maxmemory` and the memory limit have on the `cache` service
# in the application's own compose file.
VLOGS_MEMORY_LIMIT=1024M

# OPTIONAL — the host directory holding the nginx access and error logs,
# mounted read-only into Vector.
#
# nginx here is the `nginx-proxy` CONTAINER, and it writes files rather than
# stdout — which is why this source is a file tail and not another
# docker_logs include. So this must be the HOST side of that container's log
# mount. The default is what the outage runbook's `sudo grep -a
# /var/log/nginx/access.log` implies; this is the command that confirms it:
#
#   docker inspect nginx-proxy --format '{{json .Mounts}}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep -i log
NGINX_LOG_DIR=/var/log/nginx

# OPTIONAL — the collector's ceilings. The CPU one is the only such limit in
# this project: the 2026-09-13 ladders named eight cores shared by three Node
# replicas, Postgres, two Valkeys and HAProxy as the constraint, and the one
# thing Vector does that can peg a core is its first read of a large file.
VECTOR_MEMORY_LIMIT=384M
VECTOR_CPU_LIMIT=0.75


# =============================================================================
# Grafana -> the VictoriaLogs plugin
# =============================================================================
#
# The log datasource is a PLUGIN, unlike the built-in Prometheus one, so its
# provisioning can fail for a reason outside its own file: no plugin, no
# datasource.
#
# READ THE FIREWALL NOTE IN infra/README.md BEFORE CHANGING THIS.
# `DOCKER-USER` on this host is a whitelist ending in LOG + DROP, every
# dropped packet writes a kernel line `psad` reads as a port scan — the
# mechanism behind the 2026-08-30..09-05 outages — and
# GF_ANALYTICS_CHECK_FOR_UPDATES is already false, so grafana.com is a
# destination this container has NEVER reached. The install blocks startup and
# retries, so a whitelist miss is a packet storm rather than a missing panel.

# OPTIONAL — pinned, like every image tag. Set it to the EMPTY STRING to take
# the offline path below; otherwise Grafana reaches for the network even
# though the plugin is already mounted.
GRAFANA_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL=victoriametrics-logs-datasource@0.32.0

# OPTIONAL — a directory of unpacked plugins, mounted read-only. Empty in a
# deployment that downloads; holding
# ./grafana/plugins/victoriametrics-logs-datasource/ in one that cannot. Use
# the OFFICIAL release zip: it carries the MANIFEST.txt that makes the plugin
# signed, and a source build does not. Git-ignored but for its .gitkeep.
GRAFANA_PLUGINS_DIR=./grafana/plugins


# =============================================================================
# Alertmanager -> Telegram
# =============================================================================
#
# The chat id and the active receiver cannot be set from here: Alertmanager
# expands no environment variables in its own config and has no include
# directive, so there is no `receivers.yml` to point it at. What IS a variable
# is which config file gets mounted — so a deployment's own answer lives in a
# git-ignored copy rather than as an edit to a tracked file:
#
#   cp infra/alertmanager/alertmanager.yml infra/alertmanager/alertmanager.local.yml
#   # in the copy: uncomment the telegram receiver, put the real chat id in,
#   # and change `receiver:` on the route from 'null' to telegram
#   ALERTMANAGER_CONFIG=./alertmanager/alertmanager.local.yml
#
# `infra/alertmanager/*.local.yml` is git-ignored, as is the bot token in
# `infra/alertmanager/telegram-token`, so `git pull` never meets either.
#
# OPTIONAL — the default is the tracked config, which routes to the null
# receiver: alerts fire, are visible in the UI, and go no further. A relative
# path resolves against `infra/`; an absolute path outside the repository
# works too. Changing it needs `up -d alertmanager`, not `restart` — a restart
# reuses the container and with it the mount it was created with.
ALERTMANAGER_CONFIG=./alertmanager/alertmanager.yml


# =============================================================================
# Shared with the application's own .env
# =============================================================================

# REQUIRED — the database the postgres-exporter connects to, with the
# application's own role: it owns the database and may therefore read
# pg_stat_statements, which is the point of scraping it at all.
DB_USER=
DB_PASS=
DB_NAME=

# OPTIONAL — the session store's password, when it has one.
VALKEY_PASSWORD=

# OPTIONAL — the cache instance's password, when it has one.
CACHE_VALKEY_PASSWORD=
